okay today I'm going to talk to you  about limiting factors of photosynthesis  just a quick introduction photosynthesis  is the process by which plants make  their own food in the form of glucose  I've got another video that tells you  more about this but just to summarize  the plants take in carbon dioxide and  water and they produce glucose and  oxygen what we're going to look at is  three different things that can that are  limiting factors of photosynthesis so  the first one is light now each of these  limiting factors has a particular graph  that we need to know the shape of we  need to understand and be able to  explain the shape of so each time we  write rate that's rate of photosynthesis  so first of all we need to think about  light intensity now photosynthesis this  is the the word equation but we need  light in order for it to to happen so  the shape of the graph looks like this  let me explain it so it's zero light  intensity so if there's no light at all  as you can see there's no rate of  photosynthesis there will be no rate  reaction whatsoever because we need  light in order for the reaction to occur  so as we increase the light intensity  the rate of photosynthesis increases as  well so we can see it's directly  proportional up to a certain point and  then the graph levels out it reaches a  plateau now what this means is it  doesn't matter how much more we increase  the light intensity after a certain  point we reach a maximum rate of  photosynthesis so after this point here  light is no longer a limiting factor  next up what we need to do next is have  a look at carbon dioxide also known as  co2 so let's have a look for carbon  dioxide now again up the y-axis we have  rate of photosynthesis on the x-axis  we're going to show percentage level of  co2 now this graph looks pretty much  identical  to the to the last one again let's break  it down so if we've got 0% co2 so no  carbon dioxide at all we're going to  have no rate of reaction now the reason  for that is if we have a look at our  reactants we can see that carbon dioxide  is one of the reactants so if we've got  no carbon dioxide we're gonna have no  reaction whatsoever as we increase the  level of carbon dioxide the rate of  reaction is also going to increase only  up just as as the same as light up to a  certain level so after we get to this  point here we reach another plateau or  the graph stays at the same point we  reach your kind of maximum point it  means that it doesn't matter how much  more how much we increase the percentage  of carbon dioxide by we're at a maximum  rate of reaction so we can see obviously  it works best they've got a first rate  reaction a higher percentage level of  co2 but after a while we reach a plateau  again now the third one we need to know  about is temperature so the final graph  now this one looks a little bit  different than the other two so again  we've got rate of reaction here but this  time we have a graph that looks like  this  you might recognize this graph from when  we were looking at enzymes so along here  we have temperature so as we can see  initially if you've got a really low  temperature we're going to have a really  really slow rate of reaction that's  because the reactants don't have much  energy as we increase the temperature  we've given these reactants more energy  so therefore we're going to have an  increased rate of reaction all the way  up to a particular optimum now this  optimum temperature will be slightly  different depending on which plants  we're looking at and the reason for this  is as well is the  reaction for photosynthesis is  controlled by enzymes so as we increase  the temperature we're giving the  particles the reactants the enzymes more  kinetic energy so we're gonna have more  reactions sorry more frequent reactions  only up to a certain point because what  happens if you have watched my other  video about enzymes is after a certain  point the enzymes start to denature so  that means the actives they change shape  and the substrate can no longer fit in  the active site so at around this point  here at 45 degrees C normally we can see  that these enzymes have been denatured  which means we now have no rate of  reaction at all because all the enzymes  have been denatured so just to kind of  summarize the best conditions possible  for photosynthesis would be really  bright sand lots of white light high  concentration or high percentage level  of carbon dioxide and the right  temperature a little bit shorter 45  because if we go too hot the enzymes  that control photosynthesis denature  thank you
 

